00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.580
One of the challenges with building machine learning systems is that there are so many things you could try,

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:10.000
so many things you could change, including, for example, so many hyperparameters you could tune.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.560
One of the things I've noticed about the most effective machine learning people is

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:19.920
their very clear eyes about what to tune in order to try to achieve what effect.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.000
This is a process we call orthogonalization. Let me tell you what I mean.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:33.600
Here's a picture of an old-school television with a lot of knobs that you could tune to adjust the picture in various ways.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:41.600
So for these old TV sets, maybe there was one knob to adjust how tall vertically your image is,

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:48.800
another knob to adjust how wide it is, maybe another knob to adjust how neotropizoidal it is,

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:52.880
another knob to adjust how much to move the picture left and right,

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:56.960
another one to adjust how much the picture is rotated, and so on.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:03.360
And what TV designers had spent a lot of time doing was to build the circuitry,

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.160
really often analog circuitry back then, to make sure that each of the knobs had a

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.680
relatively interpretable function, such as one knob to tune this, one knob to tune this,

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:22.400
one knob to tune this, and so on. In contrast, imagine if you had a knob that tunes 0.1 times

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:31.520
how tall the image is plus 0.3 times how wide the image is minus 1.7 times how trapezoidal the image

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:40.000
is plus 0.8 times the position of the image on the horizontal axis, and so on. If you tune this knob,

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.480
then the height of the image, the width of the image, how trapezoidal it is, how much it shifts,

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:49.760
it all changes all at the same time. If you had a knob like that, it'd be almost impossible

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:53.760
to tune the TV so that the picture gets centered in the display area.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:02.800
So in this context, orthogonalization refers to that the TV designers had designed the knobs,

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:08.800
so that each knob kind of does only one thing, and this makes it much easier to tune the TV

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:16.240
so that the picture gets centered where you want it to be. Here's another example of orthogonalization.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:23.440
If you think about learning to drive a car, a car has three main controls which are steering,

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:32.000
steering wheel decides how much you go left or right, acceleration, and braking. So these three

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:39.360
controls, already one control for steering and another two controls for your speed, it makes it

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:45.840
relatively interpretable what your different actions through different controls will do to your car.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:53.200
But now imagine if someone were to build a car so that there was a joystick where one axis of

00:02:53.200 --> 00:03:01.280
the joystick controls 0.3 times your steering angle minus 0.8 times your speed, and you had

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:11.840
a different control that controls two times the steering angle plus 0.9 times the speed of your car.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:18.160
In theory, by tuning these two knobs, you could get your car to steer the angle and at the speed

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:23.200
you want, but it's much harder than if you had just one single control for controlling your

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.840
steering angle and a separate distinct set of controls for controlling your speed.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:33.280
So the concept of orthogonalization refers to that if you think of one dimension of what you

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:38.640
want to do as controlling your steering angle and another dimension as controlling your speed,

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:47.200
then you want one knob to just affect the steering angle as much as possible, and another knob in

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:52.000
the case of the car is really acceleration and braking that controls your speed. But if you have

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:57.440
the control that mixes the two together like a control like this one that affects both your

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:02.640
steering angle and your speed, something that changes both at the same time, then it becomes

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:08.080
much harder to set the car to the speed and angle you want. And by having orthogonal,

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.360
orthogonal means at 90 degrees to each other, but having orthogonal controls

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.280
that are ideally aligned with the things you actually want to control, it makes it much easier

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:23.760
to tune the knobs you have to tune to tune the steering wheel angle and your accelerator

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:31.040
you're braking to get the car to do what you want. So how does this relate to machine learning?

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:37.360
For a supervised learning system to do well, you usually need to tune the knobs of your system to

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:42.480
make sure that four things are true. First is that you usually have to make sure that you're

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:47.840
at least doing well on the training set. So performance on the training set needs to pass

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:56.000
some acceptability effecture. For some applications, this might mean doing comparably to human level

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.640
performance. But this will depend on your application. And we'll talk more about

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:07.120
comparing to human level performance next week. But after doing well on the training set,

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:14.320
you then hope that this leads to also doing well on the dev set. And you then hope that this also

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:19.600
does well on the test set. And finally, you hope that doing well on the test set and the cost

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:26.160
function results in your system performing in the real world. So you hope that this results in,

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:35.840
you know, happy cat picture app users, for example. So to relate back to the TV tuning example,

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:41.840
if the picture of your TV was either too wide or too narrow, you wanted one knob to tune in

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:46.320
order to adjust that you don't want to carefully adjust five different knobs, which are also

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:51.680
also affect different things you want one knob to just affect the width of your TV image.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:58.560
So in a similar way, if your algorithm is not fitting the training set well on the cost function,

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:03.520
you want, you know, one knob, yes, that's my attempt to draw a knob, or maybe one specific

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:09.440
set of knobs you can use to make sure you can tune your algorithm to make it fit well on the

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:15.440
training set. So the knobs you use to tune this are, you might train a bigger network,

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:23.600
or you might switch to a better optimization algorithm like the Adam optimization algorithm,

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:27.520
and so on. And there's some other options we'll discuss later this week and next week.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:34.320
In contrast, if you find that the algorithm is not fitting the dev set well, then there's a

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:39.680
separate set of knobs. I guess that's my not very artistic rendering of another knob.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:46.960
You want to have a distinct set of knobs to try. So for example, if your algorithm is not doing

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:51.200
well on the dev set, it's doing well on the training set, but not on the dev set, then you

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:56.720
have a set of knobs around regularization that you could use to try to make it satisfy the

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:02.000
second criteria. So the analogy is now, now that you've tuned the width of your TV set, if

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:07.360
the height of the image isn't quite right, then you want a different knob in order to tune the

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:12.080
height of the TV image. And you want to do this hopefully without affecting the width of your TV

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:22.400
image too much. And getting a bigger training set would be another knob you could use that helps

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:27.920
your learning algorithm generalize better to the dev set. Now having adjusted, you know,

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:33.040
the width and height of your TV image, well, what if it doesn't meet the third criteria? What if

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:39.600
you do well on the dev set, but not on the test set? If that happens, then the knob you tune is,

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:45.360
you probably want to get a bigger dev set. Because if it does well on the dev set,

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:49.600
but not on the test set, it probably means you've overtuned to a dev set, you need to go back

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:56.320
and find a bigger dev set. And finally, if it does well on the test set, but it isn't

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:03.600
delivering to you a happy cat picture app user, then what that means is that you want to go back

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:16.060
and change either the dev set or the cost function. Because if doing well on the test set

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:21.020
according to some cost function doesn't correspond to your algorithm doing what you needed to do in

00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:26.940
the real world, then it means that either your dev test set distribution isn't set correctly,

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:32.300
or your cost function isn't measuring the right thing. I know I'm going over these examples quite

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:39.500
quickly, but we'll go much more into detail on these specific knobs later this week and next week.

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:43.420
So if you aren't following all the details right now, don't worry about it. But I want to give you

00:08:43.420 --> 00:08:49.580
a sense of this authorization process that you want to be very clear about which of these maybe

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:54.700
four issues, the different things you could tune are trying to address. And when I train a neural

00:08:54.700 --> 00:09:00.780
network, I tend not to use early stopping. It's not a bad technique. Quite a lot of people do it,

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:05.900
but I personally find early stopping difficult to think about because this is a knob that

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:11.180
simultaneously affects how well you fit the training set. Because if you stop early,

00:09:11.180 --> 00:09:17.500
you fit the training set less well. But it also simultaneously is often done to improve your dev

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:22.780
set performance. So this is one knob that is you know, less orthogonalized because it

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:28.220
simultaneously affects two things. It's like a knob that simultaneously affects both the width

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:33.260
and the height of your TV image. And it doesn't mean it's a bad knob to use, you could use it if

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:38.700
you want. But when you have more orthogonalized controls, such as these other ones that I'm

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:43.420
writing down here, then it just makes the process of tuning your network much easier.

00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:48.300
So I hope that gives you a sense of what orthogonalization means. Just like when you

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:52.780
look at the TV image, it's nice if you can say, oh, my TV image is too wide, so I'm going to

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:57.340
tune this knob, or it's too tall, so I'm going to tune that knob, or it's too trapezoidal,

00:09:57.340 --> 00:10:00.860
so I'm going to have to tune that knob. In machine learning, it's nice if you can look

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:04.460
at your system and say, oh, this piece of it is wrong. It's not doing it on the training set,

00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:08.380
it's not doing it on the dev set, it's not doing it on the test set, or it's doing it on the test

00:10:08.380 --> 00:10:12.700
set, but it's not in real world. But figure out exactly what's wrong and then have exactly one

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:18.300
knob or a specific set of knobs that helps you just solve that problem that is limiting the

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:22.380
performance with the machine learning system. So what we're going to do this week and next week

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:27.980
is go through how to diagnose what exactly is the bottleneck to your system's performance,

00:10:27.980 --> 00:10:32.380
as well as identify the specific set of knobs you could use to tune your system

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:37.740
to improve that aspect of its performance. So let's start going more into the details of this process.
